  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 214 PM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\n***Thursday***\n\nGEFS and EC 500-mb height ensembles still show a broad high/ridge \nacross much of the United States Thursday morning. At the same time, \na shortwave trough traveling around the ridge anticyclonically is \nforecast to be overhead. A surface low at the leading edge of the \nshortwave will allow a cold front to traverse the Great Plains \nthroughout the day. Showers and thunderstorms are possible at the \nleading edge of the cold front, though are currently not expected to \nbe severe. LREF guidance suggests only a 25% chance or less \nthat sfc-500mb wind shear will exceed 30 kts, with the highest \nchances in Southwest Nebraska and along US-283 in Kansas. GFS \nand EC model soundings support this solution, showing less than \n30 kts of 0-6 km wind shear in this zone. However, latest GFS \nand NAMNST model soundings do show an inverted-V up to about 3 \nkm, which may indicate microburst potential with storms. \nConfidence in storm coverage is low at this time, as NBM 24 hr \nprecipitation guidance suggests only up to a 1 in 3 chance to \nreceive greater than 0.1 inches of rain across the forecast \narea. Confidence in severe weather occurring is around 5% \nThursday. \n\n\n***Friday/Saturday***\n\nThursday's cold front looks to begin eroding the strong ridge across \nthe Great Plains, moving the ridge axis westward over the Four-\nCorners Region by Friday morning. Flow at 500-mb over the \nforecast region is favored to be northwesterly to northerly\nthrough Saturday. As such, highs are forecast to slightly drop \ninto the low to mid 90s Friday, and upper 80s to lower 90s \nSaturday. A surface high in association with the ridge looks to \nbe to the north of the forecast area, and may establish \nsoutherly winds across Eastern Colorado Friday evening, with \neasterly flow across Western Kansas and Southwest Nebraska. As \nsuch, showers may develop across Eastern Colorado Friday. Again,\ncoverage is uncertain at this time, as NBM 24 hr precipitation \nguidance suggests less than a 25% chance for greater than 0.1 \ninches of rain across most of this zone. However, strengthening \nof the shortwave trough to the east may support the development \nof a 500-mb jet streak over the region. This is reflected in \nLREF sfc-500mb wind shear guidance, which shows a 40-60% chance \nof wind shear greater than 30 kts across Eastern Colorado. If \nthunderstorms are able to take advantage of this, they may be \nallowed to become severe. Even so, confidence in severe weather \nFriday evening is about 5% due to uncertainty in the coverage. \n\n\n***Sunday-Tuesday***\n\nThe ridge axis is favored to be overhead once again Sunday as a \nstrong 500-mb trough traversing Southern Canada and the Northern \nUnited States pushes it east. Conditions would warm up again into \nthe mid and upper 90s Sunday, and upper 90s to lower 100s Monday. \nHowever, conditions look to be drier. GEFS 850-mb guidance shows a \nweak low in the U.S. Southeast. This would create northerly flow \nacross the Texas coasts and prevent moisture from returning to the \nforecast region. Relative humidities (RH) may drop into the lower \n20s to upper teens during this period. While this does not meet \ncritical fire weather criteria (RH<15%), NBM guidance shows a \nbroad region across Eastern Colorado and Southwest Nebraska with\na 50% chance or higher of experiencing wind gusts over 30 mph \nboth Sunday and Monday. Considering that fuels are drier across \nthis region, the potential development of critical fire weather \nconditions may be something to keep an eye on going into the \nweekend. Additionally, a surface low associated with a trough to\nthe north may produce a few showers along a cold front Monday \nand Tuesday.",
